WEBVTT

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.314
You, like I mentioned, with SpaceX, you give a lot

00:00:06.314 --> 00:00:08.254
of people hope, and a lot of people look up

00:00:08.254 --> 00:00:10.598
to you. Millions of people look up to you. If

00:00:10.614 --> 00:00:11.893
we think about young people

00:00:12.865 --> 00:00:14.468
in high school, maybe in college,

00:00:16.053 --> 00:00:18.559
what advice would you give to them about

00:00:19.507 --> 00:00:21.403
if they wanna try to do something big in this

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.846
world, they wanna really have a big positive impact, what

00:00:23.846 --> 00:00:26.791
advice would you give them about their career, maybe about

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:27.458
life in general?

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:30.305
Try to be useful.

00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:34.449
You do things that are useful to your fellow human

00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:37.674
beings, to the world. It's very hard to be useful.

00:00:41.254 --> 00:00:41.790
Very hard.

00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:45.692
You know, are you contributing more than you consume?

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:47.912
You know, like,

00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:50.705
like, can you

00:00:52.319 --> 00:00:54.079
try to have a positive net contribution?

00:00:54.814 --> 00:00:55.429
to society.

00:00:57.886 --> 00:00:59.180
I think that's the thing to aim for.

00:00:59.972 --> 00:01:02.106
Not to try to be sort of a leader for

00:01:02.252 --> 00:01:04.552
just for the sake of being a leader or whatever.

00:01:07.473 --> 00:01:10.285
A lot of the time, people who— a lot of

00:01:10.285 --> 00:01:12.089
the time, the people you want as leaders are the

00:01:12.089 --> 00:01:13.048
people who don't want to be leaders.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:16.667
So,

00:01:19.506 --> 00:01:20.811
if you can live a useful life,

00:01:22.386 --> 00:01:23.098
that is a good life,

00:01:24.004 --> 00:01:24.457
a life worth

00:01:25.622 --> 00:01:26.075
having lived.

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:27.726
Um,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.716
you know, and I, like I said,

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.171
I would, I would encourage people to

00:01:34.199 --> 00:01:37.363
use the mental tools of physics and apply them broadly

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:37.671
in life.

00:01:38.482 --> 00:01:39.277
They are the best tools.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.879
When you think about education and self-education, what do you

00:01:42.879 --> 00:01:45.938
recommend? So there's the university, there is

00:01:48.624 --> 00:01:52.737
self-study. There is hands-on sort of finding a company or

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:54.704
a place or a set of people that do the

00:01:54.704 --> 00:01:56.704
thing you're passionate about and joining them as early as

00:01:56.737 --> 00:01:57.188
possible.

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.838
There's taking a road trip across Europe for a few

00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:06.376
years and writing some poetry. Which trajectory do you suggest?

00:02:08.548 --> 00:02:12.185
In terms of learning about how you can become useful?

00:02:12.723 --> 00:02:14.818
as you mentioned, how you can have the most positive

00:02:14.818 --> 00:02:15.052
impact?

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.717
I'd encourage people to read a lot of books.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.484
Just read, like, I mean, basically try to ingest as

00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:30.860
much information as you can

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.744
and try to also just develop a good general knowledge.

00:02:38.347 --> 00:02:39.353
so you at least have like

00:02:40.376 --> 00:02:43.005
a rough lay of the land of the knowledge landscape.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.559
Like try to learn a little bit about a lot

00:02:46.559 --> 00:02:49.580
of things, 'cause you might not know what you're really

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:51.196
interested— how would you know what you're really interested in

00:02:51.245 --> 00:02:53.261
if you at least aren't like doing a

00:02:54.049 --> 00:02:56.027
peripheral exploration broadly

00:02:57.169 --> 00:02:57.250
of

00:02:58.134 --> 00:02:59.356
the knowledge landscape?

00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:05.156
And talk to people from different walks of life and

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:05.642
different

00:03:06.983 --> 00:03:10.984
industries and professions and skills and occupations. Like, just try,

00:03:11.437 --> 00:03:11.891
you know,

00:03:13.851 --> 00:03:14.693
learn as much as possible.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.419
Man's search for meaning.

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:22.614
Isn't the whole thing a search for meaning?

00:03:24.956 --> 00:03:26.501
Yeah, what's the meaning of life and all, you know.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.302
But just generally, like I said, I would encourage people

00:03:29.302 --> 00:03:30.155
to read broadly

00:03:31.137 --> 00:03:32.328
in many different subject areas.

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:38.356
And then try to find something where there's an overlap

00:03:38.596 --> 00:03:42.525
of your talents and what you're interested in. So people

00:03:42.782 --> 00:03:45.621
may be good at something, or they may have skill

00:03:45.733 --> 00:03:47.513
at a particular thing, but they don't like doing it.

00:03:47.914 --> 00:03:48.684
Mm-hmm.

00:03:49.678 --> 00:03:52.917
So you wanna try to find a thing where you're—

00:03:52.949 --> 00:03:57.584
that's a good combination of the things that you're inherently

00:03:57.616 --> 00:03:59.669
good at, but you also like doing.

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:06.568
And reading is a super fast shortcut to figure out

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:09.620
which, where are you. You're both good at it, you

00:04:09.717 --> 00:04:12.789
like doing it, and it will actually have positive impact.

00:04:13.418 --> 00:04:15.885
Well, you gotta learn about things somehow. So, reading

00:04:17.466 --> 00:04:19.369
a broad range, just really

00:04:20.523 --> 00:04:23.449
read it, you know. One point when I was a

00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:25.628
kid, I read through the encyclopedia.

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.150
So, that was pretty helpful.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.570
And also things I didn't even know existed, law, law,

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.670
lots, obviously. It's like as broad as it gets. Encyclopedias

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:41.170
were digestible, I think, whatever, 40 years ago.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:48.410
So, maybe read through the condensed version of the Encyclopedia

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.010
Britannica. I'd recommend that.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.570
You can always skip subjects where you read a few

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.910
paragraphs and you know you're not interested, just jump to

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:55.970
the next one.

00:04:57.659 --> 00:05:01.519
So, read the encyclopedia or skim through it.

00:05:07.618 --> 00:05:09.375
But I put a lot of stock in, certainly have

00:05:09.569 --> 00:05:12.471
a lot of respect for someone who puts in an

00:05:12.471 --> 00:05:13.212
honest day's work

00:05:14.002 --> 00:05:14.776
to do useful things.

00:05:16.958 --> 00:05:18.192
And just generally to have

00:05:18.906 --> 00:05:20.237
not a zero-sum mindset

00:05:21.666 --> 00:05:21.715
or

00:05:22.868 --> 00:05:23.452
have more of a

00:05:24.444 --> 00:05:28.936
grow-the-pie mindset. If you sort of say,

00:05:29.818 --> 00:05:34.054
when we see people, perhaps including some very smart people,

00:05:35.418 --> 00:05:37.504
kind of taking an attitude of

00:05:38.386 --> 00:05:40.279
doing things that seem morally questionable,

00:05:41.194 --> 00:05:44.852
it's often because they have at a base axiomatic level

00:05:45.461 --> 00:05:46.568
a zero-sum mindset.

00:05:47.788 --> 00:05:48.413
Right.

00:05:49.394 --> 00:05:52.382
without realizing it. They don't realize they have a zero-sum

00:05:52.382 --> 00:05:55.012
mindset, or at least they don't realize it consciously.

00:05:56.032 --> 00:05:57.922
And so if you have a zero-sum mindset, then the

00:05:57.922 --> 00:05:59.828
only way to get ahead is by taking things from

00:05:59.828 --> 00:06:00.038
others.

00:06:02.753 --> 00:06:06.100
If the pie is fixed, then the only way to

00:06:06.261 --> 00:06:08.369
have more pie is to take someone else's pie.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.419
But this is false. Obviously the pie has grown dramatically

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:13.533
over time, the economic pie.

00:06:14.744 --> 00:06:17.137
So In reality, you can have—

00:06:19.831 --> 00:06:22.283
I overuse this analogy— you can have a lot of—

00:06:22.751 --> 00:06:26.961
there's a lot of pie. Yeah, absolutely. Pie is not

00:06:26.978 --> 00:06:27.224
fixed.

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:31.624
So, you really want to make sure you're not operating

00:06:33.169 --> 00:06:37.033
without realizing it from a zero-sum mindset where the only

00:06:37.049 --> 00:06:38.353
way to get ahead is to take things from others.

00:06:38.418 --> 00:06:40.104
Then that's going to result in you trying to take

00:06:40.121 --> 00:06:42.348
things from others, which is not Not good. It's much

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:43.075
better to work on

00:06:44.222 --> 00:06:46.386
adding to the economic pie.

00:06:49.423 --> 00:06:51.007
So, creating,

00:06:52.333 --> 00:06:53.950
like I said, creating more than you consume,

00:06:54.874 --> 00:06:58.880
doing more than you— yeah. So, that's a big deal.

00:06:59.578 --> 00:07:01.816
I think there's a fair number of people in

00:07:03.072 --> 00:07:03.983
finance that

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:07.650
do have a bit of a zero-sum mindset. I mean,

00:07:07.666 --> 00:07:10.855
it's all walks of life. I've seen that. One of

00:07:11.129 --> 00:07:11.500
the reasons

00:07:13.368 --> 00:07:17.219
Rogan inspires me is he celebrates others a lot. There's

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:20.558
not creating a constant competition. Like, there's a scarcity of

00:07:20.558 --> 00:07:23.946
resources. What happens when you celebrate others and you promote

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:24.317
others,

00:07:25.106 --> 00:07:26.054
the ideas of others,

00:07:27.195 --> 00:07:30.650
it actually grows that pie. I mean, it every— like,

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:30.923
the

00:07:31.774 --> 00:07:36.137
resources become less scarce. And that applies in a lot

00:07:36.137 --> 00:07:38.016
of kinds of domains. It applies in academia where a

00:07:38.016 --> 00:07:39.076
lot of people are very,

00:07:39.911 --> 00:07:43.748
see some funding for academic research as zero-sum, and it

00:07:43.748 --> 00:07:45.835
is not. If you celebrate each other, if you make,

00:07:46.269 --> 00:07:48.421
if you get everybody to be excited about AI, about

00:07:48.421 --> 00:07:51.889
physics, about mathematics, I think there'd be more and more

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:54.907
funding, and I think everybody wins. Yeah, that applies I

00:07:54.907 --> 00:07:55.534
think broadly.

00:07:56.834 --> 00:07:58.295
Yeah, yeah, exactly.
